Original commit body from cbb6b6a9a4b3e9a20dde71695a847799e849a7be:

`derive_silence_reason` deliberately never infers NO_CANDIDATE_CONSTRUCT --
only a pass BODY knows whether it looked for its construct and failed to find
one, and a reason invented on the producer's behalf is a fabricated
disclosure. But both stamp sites then assigned the derived value
UNCONDITIONALLY, overwriting the one producer able to say it. The declared
value was unreachable from anywhere in the tree: a vocabulary entry with no
possible producer, which is the instrument-with-no-reader defect wearing a
vocabulary's clothes.

Both chokepoints now FILL rather than OVERRULE, matching how duration_ms and
files_analyzed are already guarded at these same two sites. The one exception
is "": a pass that EMITTED has no silence to explain, so NOT APPLICABLE still
overrides a stale or mistaken body value rather than publishing a
contradiction.

Six tests, three per chokepoint: a body-supplied reason survives; it survives
even a zero file count (outranking the orchestrator's no_candidate_files); and
a pass that emitted is never silent whatever the body said. The last of those
already passed -- it pins behaviour the guard must not break.

SCOPE, STATED: this makes the value REACHABLE, not REACHED. No production body
claims it yet, so the eleven `unreported` pass-runs are unchanged. Draining
them is per-linker judgement: a linker emitting a symbol per candidate is
silent exactly when it found none, but a linker emitting only PAIRING edges is
also silent when it found candidates on one side and none on the other.
crypto-flow and message-dispatch both bail on `if not all_writes or not
all_reads`, which conflates those two; stamping that whole branch would
install a fresh false claim in the axis built to cure false claims.

